./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:1:# Field Name Aliases in Schema-Once Output: Do They Save Tokens?
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:11:We investigated whether registering short aliases for field names (e.g., `status` -> `s`, `assignee` -> `a`) would meaningfully reduce token consumption in agent-facing CLI output that already uses a schema-once compact format. Three independent studies measured: (1) raw token savings from abbreviation across payload scales, (2) LLM comprehension impact at 5, 15, and 30 alias complexity levels, and (3) session-level token economics including schema discovery roundtrip overhead.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:13:**Finding: aliases are not worth implementing.** In compact tabular (schema-once) format, field names appear exactly once in the header row. Abbreviating the header saves a fixed 5 tokens regardless of whether the payload contains 5 or 500 items — a 0.02% to 1.86% marginal reduction. Meanwhile, the alias dictionary requires a `schema()` introspection roundtrip costing 85 tokens per call, producing a net token loss in 75% of simulated session scenarios. The compact format already solves the problem aliases target (repeated key names), making aliases architecturally redundant.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:21:When AI agents consume structured data from CLI tools, token efficiency directly impacts cost and context window utilization. A well-optimized agent-facing query layer implements several techniques to minimize output tokens:
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:23:1. **Field projection** — return only requested fields (~variable savings)
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:24:2. **Format switch** — compact tabular output instead of JSON (~46% savings)
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:25:3. **Batching** — multiple queries per tool call (~80 tokens saved per avoided call)
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:26:4. **Presets** — named field bundles reduce query input tokens
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:28:After implementing these four optimizations, a natural question arises: can we squeeze out more tokens by abbreviating field names themselves?
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:32:Field name aliases (`id` -> `i`, `name` -> `n`, `status` -> `s`) should reduce output tokens because shorter strings produce fewer tokens. The alias mapping would be stored in the schema and exposed via a `schema()` introspection call. The agent learns the dictionary once, then reads abbreviated output for the remainder of the session.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:36:Three potential costs could negate the savings:
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:38:1. **Token savings might be trivial** if field names are already short or appear infrequently in the output format
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:40:3. **Schema roundtrip overhead** (the cost of calling `schema()` to learn the alias dictionary) might exceed the per-query savings, especially if context compression forces repeated re-learning
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:46:## 2. Study 1: Token Savings Measurement
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:58:Token counts measured with `tiktoken` using `cl100k_base` encoding (compatible with GPT-4 and Claude tokenizers). Random seed fixed at 42 for reproducibility. Payloads used realistic data: task names, assignee names, ISO date strings, multi-word descriptions.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:62:#### Raw Token Counts
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:71:#### Savings Breakdown
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:77:| **Absolute alias savings** | **5 tok** | **5 tok** | **5 tok** | **5 tok** |
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:81:The alias savings are constant at 5 tokens because in CSV-style output, field names appear **exactly once** — in the header row. The full header:
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:87:is 14 tokens. The abbreviated header:
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:93:is 9 tokens. The difference (5 tokens) is fixed regardless of how many data rows follow. All data rows are identical in both variants — they contain values, not keys.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:106:JSON costs ~98 tokens per item (keys repeat per object). Compact costs ~53 tokens per item (keys declared once). Aliases save an additional 1 token per item at 5 items, converging to 0 per item at scale.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:168:4. **Partial context eviction** — the worst failure mode. After context compression, the agent remembers some aliases but not others, silently applying incorrect mappings.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:174:## 4. Study 3: Session-Level Token Economics
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:178:All token counts measured with `tiktoken` cl100k_base encoding on real CLI output from an agentquery-based task tracker:
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:180:| Metric | Tokens | Method |
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:183:| Alias savings per query (compact) | 5 | Fixed: header abbreviation only |
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:184:| Alias savings per query (JSON get) | 3 | Abbreviated object keys |
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:185:| Alias savings per query (JSON list, 10 items) | 30 | 3 tokens/item x 10 |
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:193:| Session | Eviction K | Schema Calls | Schema Cost | Alias Savings | **Net** |
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:211:queries_between_evictions > schema_cost / savings_per_query
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:214:| Format | Savings/Query | Schema Cost | Break-Even |
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:220:For compact format, the agent needs 17 consecutive data queries between schema refreshes — unreliable given typical context eviction patterns (every 10-20 turns in production agent systems after ~180K context tokens).
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:235:**Batching comparison:** For the "check status of 5 tasks" workflow, batching 5 queries into a single call costs 165 tokens — 33% less than individual queries (246 tok) and 46% less than aliases (306 tok). Batching is the superior optimization with zero overhead.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:239:Aliases in JSON format *are* economical — saving ~3 tokens per item per query, easily exceeding the 85-token schema cost in moderate sessions. But this finding is irrelevant: the recommendation is to use compact format, which already saves ~46% vs JSON. Compact format eliminates the key repetition that makes JSON aliases valuable. **The two optimizations are substitutes, not complements.** Implementing aliases to optimize JSON output is like tuning a carburetor after installing fuel injection.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:255:| Rank | Optimization | Savings | Discovery Overhead |
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:261:| **5** | **Field aliases** | **5 tokens (fixed)** | **85 tok per schema() call** |
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:265:### BPE Tokenizers Already Compress Field Names
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:267:A second-order insight: modern BPE tokenizers already perform the compression that aliases attempt. Common field names — "status", "name", "id", "type" — are single tokens in cl100k_base. Abbreviating `status` (1 token) to `s` (1 token) saves zero tokens. Only multi-token field names benefit: `blocked_by` (3 tokens) -> `bb` (1 token) saves 2 tokens. But these savings only manifest in JSON (where keys repeat per item), not in compact format (where the header appears once).
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:286:**TOON format** uses full field names in its schema headers despite being designed explicitly for LLM token efficiency. TOON achieves 73.9% accuracy (vs 69.7% for JSON) with 39.6% fewer tokens through structural clarity, not name abbreviation. This validates the approach: **structure matters more than name length.**
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:294:Field name aliases in schema-once output are a solution to a problem that no longer exists. The compact tabular format — by declaring field names once in a header row — already eliminates the per-item key repetition that aliases target. The marginal savings (5 tokens, fixed) are dwarfed by the schema discovery overhead (85 tokens per roundtrip), producing a net token loss in 75% of simulated scenarios.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:296:The token optimization hierarchy for agent-facing output is clear: field projection and compact format deliver the largest gains (46%+) with zero overhead. Batching and presets provide additional savings. Aliases sit at the bottom — the only optimization where discovery cost exceeds the benefit in typical usage.
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:302:| Net token savings | > 10% | 0.02-1.86% (5 tokens fixed) | FAIL |
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:309:1. **Compact format adoption** — the 46% savings is the single biggest win available
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:310:2. **Batch query utilization** — agents underutilize multi-query syntax, leaving ~80 tokens per call on the table
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:312:4. **Value-level compression** — shortening date formats, status codes (warranting separate measurement)
./articles/field-alias-compression-study.md:320:2. TOON: Token-Oriented Object Notation. [github.com/toon-format/toon](https://github.com/toon-format/toon)
