./references/comparison-example.md:92:If we include DSL syntax documentation in the system prompt (the `--help` text is ~500 bytes), that's ~125 tokens -- about 18x smaller than MCP tool definitions.
./references/comparison-example.md:96:Same as DSL. No tool definitions. Usage is discoverable via `task-board grep --help` (~200 bytes / ~50 tokens).
./references/comparison-example.md:106:The question: **At how many queries does MCP's session overhead (2,200 tokens) pay for itself vs DSL?**
./references/comparison-example.md:108:### Per-query savings: MCP vs DSL
./references/comparison-example.md:110:MCP and DSL produce identical JSON output. The only per-query differences are:
./references/comparison-example.md:112:| Factor | MCP | DSL | Delta |
./references/comparison-example.md:114:| Input size | Slightly smaller (JSON vs command string) | Slightly larger | ~5-10 tokens MCP advantage |
./references/comparison-example.md:119:**Single-query delta:** MCP saves ~5-10 tokens per query on input.
./references/comparison-example.md:124:- DSL batch of 3: 144 tokens (1 call)
./references/comparison-example.md:125:- MCP batch of 3: 338 tokens (3 calls)
./references/comparison-example.md:126:- **DSL saves ~194 tokens per 3-element batch**
./references/comparison-example.md:128:With batching factored in, MCP **never breaks even** against DSL for typical agent sessions.
./references/comparison-example.md:130:### MCP vs CLI
./references/comparison-example.md:132:CLI text output is sometimes smaller (W1: 179 vs 366 tokens) and sometimes larger (W5: 543 vs 34 tokens). The key advantage of structured approaches (MCP/DSL) is **field selection** -- the agent can request only what it needs.
./references/comparison-example.md:135:- `get(X) { minimal }` = 20 tokens vs `show X` = 164 tokens
./references/comparison-example.md:136:- Savings: **144 tokens per lookup**
./references/comparison-example.md:137:- Break-even for MCP vs CLI: 2,200 / 144 = **~15 queries per session**
./references/comparison-example.md:139:### MCP vs Grep
./references/comparison-example.md:141:Grep is so unreliable (fails for W2, 11K tokens for W4) that break-even analysis is meaningless. Grep loses on every workflow where the board has more than a few elements.
./references/comparison-example.md:151:| Criterion | DSL | MCP | CLI | Grep |
./references/comparison-example.md:167:**MCP (specific niche: long-lived sessions with external tool orchestration):**
./references/comparison-example.md:168:- When the agent platform natively supports MCP and tool switching overhead matters more than context size
./references/comparison-example.md:169:- When the session is so long that 2,200 tokens of overhead becomes negligible
./references/comparison-example.md:170:- When other MCP servers are already in use (marginal cost of adding one more is lower)
./references/comparison-example.md:186:The Mini-Query DSL is the clear winner for agent workflows: it has zero session overhead (vs MCP's 2,200 tokens), supports batch queries in a single tool call (saving ~194 tokens per 3-element batch vs MCP's 3 separate calls), and produces the same structured JSON output as MCP. Board Grep is a niche tool for unstructured text search that fails catastrophically on structured queries (11K tokens for agent monitoring vs DSL's 686). MCP only becomes competitive in sessions exceeding ~300 queries, a threshold that typical agent tasks never reach.
./references/comparison-example.md:190:## Appendix: Token Estimation Calibration
./references/comparison-example.md:192:Token estimates used in this document are approximations. Actual token counts vary by model tokenizer. The ratios used:
./references/comparison-example.md:194:| Content type | Bytes per token | Rationale |
./references/comparison-example.md:196:| English text (CLI output) | 4.0 | Standard English tokenization |
./references/comparison-example.md:197:| JSON (DSL/MCP output) | 3.0 | More punctuation characters, shorter key names |
./references/comparison-example.md:201:For production use, these should be validated against the actual tokenizer (cl100k_base for Claude models).
