 130  
 131  | Level | Fields | Abbreviated | Full | Delta |
 132  |-------|-------:|:-----------:|:----:|:-----:|
 133  | 1 | 5 | 10/10 (100%) | 10/10 (100%) | 0% |
 134  | 2 | 15 | 10/10 (100%) | 10/10 (100%) | 0% |
 135  | 3 | 30 | 10/10 (100%) | 10/10 (100%) | 0% |
 136  
 137  **Zero accuracy degradation across all levels** when an explicit dictionary is provided.
 138  
 139  ### Qualitative Friction
 140  
 141  Despite perfect accuracy, aliases introduced measurable cognitive overhead at Level 3. Collision clusters required repeated dictionary lookups:
 142  
 143  - **The "s-family":** `s`=status, `sc`=scope, `sp`=sprint, `st`=story-points, `sr`=source
 144  - **The "c-family":** `c`=category, `cl`=closed, `cm`=comments, `cr`=created
 145  - **The "r-family":** `r`=reporter, `rv`=reviewer, `rn`=rank
 146  
 147  **Processing path comparison** (Level 3, Q2: "What sprint and scope does T-209 belong to?"):
 148  
 149  | Step | Full Names | Abbreviated |
 150  |------|-----------|-------------|
 151  | 1 | Find T-209 row | Lookup: sp=sprint, sc=scope (not s, not st) |
 152  | 2 | Read "sprint" column -> S-15 | Find T-209 row |
 153  | 3 | Read "scope" column -> backend | Locate `sp` column (16th) -> S-15 |
 154  | 4 | — | Locate `sc` column (15th) -> backend |
 155  
 156  The abbreviated path adds one dictionary lookup per aliased field. Trivial for a single question. Over thousands of agent queries, this compounds.
 157  
 158  ### Failure Modes
 159  
 160  Five failure modes were identified for production deployment:
 161  
 162  1. **No dictionary provided** — catastrophic. `c` could mean category, comments, created, or closed. The Columbo study (EMNLP 2025) measured 10.54% NL2SQL accuracy drop and 40.5% relation detection drop with undocumented abbreviations.
 163  
 164  2. **Domain-specific ambiguity** — `s` defaults to "status" in developer tools but could mean "story", "sprint", "severity" in other contexts. Domain priors may override dictionary lookup.
 165  
 166  3. **Multi-schema contexts** — agent works with multiple tools using different alias dictionaries. `p`=priority in Tool A, `p`=parent in Tool B. Cross-contamination is likely.
 167  
 168  4. **Partial context eviction** — the worst failure mode. After context compression, the agent remembers some aliases but not others, silently applying incorrect mappings.
 169  
 170  5. **Aggregation at scale** — counting or filtering over 100+ rows while holding alias mappings strains attention, though this is a general LLM limitation orthogonal to aliasing.
 171  
 172  ---
 173  
 174  ## 4. Study 3: Session-Level Token Economics
 175  
 176  ### Measured Constants
 177  
 178  All token counts measured with `tiktoken` cl100k_base encoding on real CLI output from an agentquery-based task tracker:
 179  
 180  | Metric | Tokens | Method |
 181  |--------|-------:|--------|
 182  | Schema() roundtrip cost | 85 | 10 (call) + 71 (response) + 4 (overhead) |
 183  | Alias savings per query (compact) | 5 | Fixed: header abbreviation only |
 184  | Alias savings per query (JSON get) | 3 | Abbreviated object keys |
 185  | Alias savings per query (JSON list, 10 items) | 30 | 3 tokens/item x 10 |
 186  | Typical get() response (compact) | 23 | Real CLI measurement |
 187  | Typical list() response, 8 items (compact) | 177 | Real CLI measurement |
 188  
 189  ### Session Simulation
 190  
 191  Modeled agent sessions of varying length with context eviction — the dictionary is forgotten every K turns, requiring a `schema()` re-query:
 192  
 193  | Session | Eviction K | Schema Calls | Schema Cost | Alias Savings | **Net** |
 194  |--------:|-----------:|-------------:|------------:|--------------:|--------:|
 195  | 10 | 10 | 1 | 85 | 40 | **-45** |
 196  | 10 | never | 1 | 85 | 40 | **-45** |
 197  | 20 | 10 | 2 | 170 | 80 | **-90** |
 198  | 20 | 20 | 1 | 85 | 80 | **-5** |
 199  | 50 | 10 | 5 | 425 | 200 | **-225** |
 200  | 50 | 50 | 1 | 85 | 200 | **+115** |
 201  | 100 | 10 | 10 | 850 | 400 | **-450** |
 202  | 100 | 20 | 5 | 425 | 400 | **-25** |
 203  | 100 | 50 | 2 | 170 | 400 | **+230** |
 204  | 100 | never | 1 | 85 | 400 | **+315** |
 205  
 206  **Net positive in only 4 of 16 scenarios (25%).** All require either long sessions (50+ queries) with infrequent eviction (50+ turns), or the unrealistic assumption that the dictionary is never evicted from context.
 207  
 208  ### Break-Even Analysis
 209  
 210  ```
 211  queries_between_evictions > schema_cost / savings_per_query
 212  ```
 213  
 214  | Format | Savings/Query | Schema Cost | Break-Even |
 215  |--------|-------------:|------------:|-----------:|
 216  | Compact | 5 | 85 | **17 queries** |
 217  | JSON (get) | 3 | 85 | **29 queries** |
 218  | JSON (list, 10 items) | 30 | 85 | **3 queries** |
 219  
 220  For compact format, the agent needs 17 consecutive data queries between schema refreshes — unreliable given typical context eviction patterns (every 10-20 turns in production agent systems after ~180K context tokens).
 221  
 222  ### Workflow Analysis
 223  
 224  Four representative agent workflows modeled end-to-end:
 225  
 226  | Workflow | Without Aliases | With Aliases | Delta |
 227  |----------|---------------:|-------------:|------:|
 228  | Check status of 5 tasks | 246 tok | 306 tok | **+24% worse** |
 229  | Find blocked tasks + update | 486 tok | 541 tok | **+11% worse** |
 230  | Daily standup review | 130 tok | 196 tok | **+51% worse** |
 231  | Heavy analytics (100q, evict/20) | ~6,000 tok | ~6,025 tok | **-0.4% marginal loss** |
 232  
 233  Every workflow shows aliases performing equal to or worse than the no-alias baseline.
 234  
 235  **Batching comparison:** For the "check status of 5 tasks" workflow, batching 5 queries into a single call costs 165 tokens — 33% less than individual queries (246 tok) and 46% less than aliases (306 tok). Batching is the superior optimization with zero overhead.
 236  
 237  ### The JSON Alias Irony
 238  
 239  Aliases in JSON format *are* economical — saving ~3 tokens per item per query, easily exceeding the 85-token schema cost in moderate sessions. But this finding is irrelevant: the recommendation is to use compact format, which already saves ~46% vs JSON. Compact format eliminates the key repetition that makes JSON aliases valuable. **The two optimizations are substitutes, not complements.** Implementing aliases to optimize JSON output is like tuning a carburetor after installing fuel injection.
 240  
 241  ---
 242  
 243  ## 5. Discussion
 244  
 245  ### The Core Insight: Schema-Once Kills Aliases
 246  
 247  The three studies converge on a single structural insight:
 248  
 249  > **In any schema-once output format (CSV, TSV, TOON), field names appear exactly once. Abbreviating a one-time occurrence produces a one-time saving. As payload size grows, the marginal value of that saving approaches zero.**
 250  
 251  This is not a limitation of a specific implementation — it's an inherent property of the format. Any compact tabular format with a header row followed by value rows exhibits this behavior. Aliases are a solution to key repetition, and schema-once formats have no key repetition left to solve.
 252  
 253  ### The Optimization Hierarchy
 254  
 255  | Rank | Optimization | Savings | Discovery Overhead |
 256  |------|-------------|---------|-------------------|
 257  | 1 | Field projection | 50-80% (selective queries) | Zero |
 258  | 2 | Compact format (JSON -> tabular) | ~46% | Zero |
 259  | 3 | Batching | ~80 tok per avoided call | Zero |
 260  | 4 | Presets | ~5-10 tok per query input | Zero |

