   1  # Agent-Facing API
   2  
   3  Design pattern for building agent-optimized CLI query layers. Two-layer read approach that minimizes token overhead without adding infrastructure.
   4  
   5  ## The Problem
   6  
   7  CLI tools built for humans produce verbose, formatted output with ANSI colors, alignment padding, and all fields included. When AI agents consume this output, they pay 1.5-5x more tokens than necessary. MCP servers fix the format but add ~2,200 tokens of session overhead (tool definitions) and don't support batching.
   8  
   9  ## The Solution
  10  
  11  Two read layers on top of your existing CLI:
  12  
  13  | Layer | Purpose | Output | Example |
  14  |-------|---------|--------|---------|
  15  | **Mini-Query DSL** | Structured reads | JSON or compact tabular | `mytool q 'get(ID) { status assignee }'` |
  16  | **Mutations** | Structured writes | JSON or compact tabular | `mytool m 'update(ID, status=done)' --confirm` |
  17  | **Scoped Grep** | Full-text search | JSON or grouped-by-file text | `mytool grep "pattern" --file progress.md` |
  18  
  19  Writes use the same DSL grammar via a dedicated `m` subcommand with safety flags (`--confirm`, `--dry-run`). No new processes, no session overhead, no extra tool definitions.
  20  
  21  ### Why Not MCP?
  22  
  23  - **Session overhead:** ~2,200 tokens of tool definitions loaded per session
  24  - **No batching:** each query is a separate tool call (~80 tok framing each)
  25  - **Break-even:** ~293 queries per session (unrealistic for typical agent work)
  26  - **Identical output:** MCP and DSL return the same JSON when backed by the same field selection engine
  27  
  28  See [references/comparison-example.md](references/comparison-example.md) for a detailed measurement on a real 346-element board.
  29  
  30  ## Design Principle: Format Is a Transport Concern
  31  
  32  The source of data (Schema, database, API) must never decide how that data is serialized. The **caller** declares the format, always, at every layer.
  33  
  34  This is the only correct way to pipe data from any source to any consumer. If you bake the format into the source, you lock out every other consumer:
  35  
  36  | Layer | How the caller declares format |
  37  |-------|-------------------------------|
  38  | CLI | `--format compact` / `--format json` (required flag, no default) |
  39  | SDK (per-call) | `QueryJSONWithMode(query, LLMReadable)` |
  40  | REST API | `Accept: application/json` header |
  41  | gRPC | Request field: `output_format: COMPACT` |
  42  
  43  The source stays format-agnostic. It returns structured data; the transport layer serializes it for the consumer. A TUI app, an AI agent, and a human all call the same CLI; they only differ in `--format`.
  44  
  45  **Anti-pattern:** configuring output format at schema/source initialization (e.g., `NewSchema(WithOutputMode(...))`). This couples the data model to a single consumer. When a second consumer appears with different needs, you're stuck.
  46  
  47  ## Output Modes
  48  
  49  The `--format` flag (required on CLI commands) controls output serialization:
  50  
  51  | `--format` | Mode | Format | Token cost |
  52  |------------|------|--------|------------|
  53  | `json` | `HumanReadable` | Standard JSON | Baseline |
  54  | `compact` / `llm` | `LLMReadable` | Compact tabular text | ~30-50% fewer tokens |
  55  
  56  Format is a **caller decision** (transport concern), not a schema setting. The same CLI tool serves different consumers: agents pass `--format compact`, TUI apps and humans pass `--format json`.
  57  
  58  **Compact output examples:**
  59  
  60  ```
  61  # List: CSV-style header + rows
  62  id,name,status,assignee
  63  task-1,Auth service refactor,in-progress,alice
  64  task-2,Dashboard performance,todo,bob
  65  
  66  # Single element: key:value pairs
  67  id:task-1
  68  name:Auth service refactor
  69  status:in-progress
  70  
  71  # Search: grouped by file
  72  README.md
  73    3: matching line
  74    4  context line
  75  ```
  76  
  77  ## Quick Start
  78  
  79  Read `SKILL.md` for the full pattern specification:
  80  
  81  - **Layer 1: DSL**: syntax template, implementation checklist, token budgets
  82  - **Layer 2: Grep**: scoping, filters, when to use vs DSL
  83  - **Layer 3: CLI**: writes stay as commands
  84  - **Anti-patterns**: common mistakes and their costs
  85  - **Implementation guide**: architecture, parser tips, Go project structure
  86  
  87  ## Mutations (Write Operations)
  88  
  89  Mutations use the same DSL grammar as queries; no new syntax. They're registered separately via `Mutation()`/`MutationWithMetadata()` and accessed through the `m` subcommand:
  90  
  91  ```bash
  92  # Create
  93  mytool m 'create(title="Fix bug", status=todo)' --format json
  94  
  95  # Update (positional ID)
  96  mytool m 'update(item-1, status=done)' --format json
  97  
  98  # Delete (destructive, requires --confirm)
  99  mytool m 'delete(item-1)' --format json --confirm
 100  
 101  # Dry run (preview without applying)
 102  mytool m 'delete(item-1)' --format json --dry-run
 103  ```
 104  
 105  **Safety flags:**
 106  - `--confirm`: required for mutations marked `Destructive: true`
 107  - `--dry-run`: injects `dry_run=true` into parsed AST statements; quoted separators remain payload text
 108  - Schema validation rejects unknown named arguments and unexpected positional fragments with typed mutation errors before handlers run.
 109  
 110  For transports that inspect a request before execution, parse once with
 111  `Schema.Parse()` and execute the same AST through `QueryAST()` or
 112  `QueryJSONASTWithMode()`. `Render()` provides canonical DSL text when an AST
 113  must be forwarded to another process or service.
 114  
 115  **MutationContext convenience methods** reduce handler boilerplate:
 116  
 117  ```go
 118  func myHandler(ctx agentquery.MutationContext[Item]) (any, error) {
 119      id := ctx.PositionalArg()           // first keyless arg (e.g. "item-1" from "update(item-1, ...)")
 120      status, err := ctx.RequireArg("status") // named arg, error if missing
 121      priority := ctx.ArgDefault("priority", "medium") // named arg with default
 122      // ...
 123  }
 124  ```
 125  
 126  **Schema introspection** includes separate `mutations` and `mutationMetadata` sections, so agents clearly see the read/write boundary:
 127  
 128  ```json
 129  {
